<document xmlns="http://cnx.rice.edu/cnxml" xmlns:m="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML">
  <title>5.1.2: Using Product and Quotient Properties for Exponents</title>
  <metadata xmlns:md="http://cnx.rice.edu/mdml">
    <md:content-id>m01228</md:content-id>
    <md:title>5.1.2: Using Product and Quotient Properties for Exponents</md:title>
    <md:uuid>6653bfab-56d4-43ff-85d4-fdb1660d081c</md:uuid>
  </metadata>
  <content>
  <section><title>Activity (20 minutes)</title>
<para>This activity prompts students to simplify expressions using the Product and Quotient Properties for Exponents.
  Students must understand that to multiply like bases, the exponents are added and to divide like bases, the exponents
  are subtracted. The base in these situations always remains the same.</para>
<title>Launch</title>
<para>Read the Product Property written in the middle column. Review the example provided as a class. If needed, complete
  one additional row in the table as a class.</para>
<para>Limit the time spent on the first table to 10 minutes and pause the class for questions before students move on to
  the second table.</para>
<para>Then have students complete the second table. Read the Quotient Property written in the middle column. Review the
  example provided as a class. If needed, complete one additional row in the table as a class.</para>
<title>Student Activity</title>
<para> Double click on mathematical expressions/equations to enlarge.</para>
<title>Activity</title>
<note>
  <para class="os-raise-text-bold">Product Property for Exponents</para>
    <hr/>
  <para>If \(a\) is a real number and \(m\) and \(n\) are integers, then</para>
  <para>\(a^m \cdot a^n=a^{m+n}\) </para>
  <para>To multiply with like bases, add the exponents.</para>
</note>
<para>Complete problems 1 - 6 using the product property for exponents. Double click on mathematical expressions/equations
  to enlarge.</para>
<list class="os-raise-noindent" type="enumerated">
  <item> \(d^2 \cdot d^3\) </item>
</list>
<para class="os-raise-text-bold">Answer:</para>
<para>\(d^2 \cdot d^3 = d^{2+3} = d^5\)</para>
<list class="os-raise-noindent" start="2" type="enumerated">
  <item> \(c^4 \cdot c^6\) </item>
</list>
<para class="os-raise-text-bold">Answer:</para>
<para>\(c^4 \cdot c^6 = c^{4+6} = c^{10}\)</para>
<list class="os-raise-noindent" start="3" type="enumerated">
  <item> \(k^3 \cdot k^5\) </item>
</list>
<para class="os-raise-text-bold">Answer:</para>
<para>\(k^3 \cdot k^5 = k^{3+5} = k^8\)</para>
<list class="os-raise-noindent" start="4" type="enumerated">
  <item> \((-v)^2 \cdot (-v)^1\) </item>
</list>
<para class="os-raise-text-bold">Answer:</para>
<para>\((-v)^2 \cdot (-v)^1 = (-v)^{2+1} = (-v)^3\)</para>
<list class="os-raise-noindent" start="5" type="enumerated">
  <item> \((m)^4 \cdot (m)^4\) </item>
</list>
<para class="os-raise-text-bold">Answer:</para>
<para>\((m)^4 \cdot (m)^4 = m^{4+4} = m^8\)</para>
<list class="os-raise-noindent" start="6" type="enumerated">
  <item> \((-p)^6 \cdot (-p)^8\) </item>
</list>
<para class="os-raise-text-bold">Answer:</para>
<para>\((-p)^6 \cdot (-p)^8 = (-p)^{6+8} = (-p)^{14}\)</para>
<note>
  <para class="os-raise-text-bold">Product Quotient for Exponents</para>
    <hr/>
  <para>If \(a\) is a real number, \(a\neq0\), and \(m\) and \(n\) are integers, then</para>
  <para>\(\frac{a^m}{a^n}=a^{m-n}\), \(m&gt;n\) and \(\frac{a^m}{a^n}=\frac1{a^{n-m}}\), \(n&gt;m\) </para>
  <para>To divide with like bases, subtract the exponents.</para>
</note>
<para>Complete problems 7 - 12 using the quotient property for exponents. Double click on mathematical
  expressions/equations to enlarge.</para>
<list class="os-raise-noindent" start="7" type="enumerated">
  <item> \(\frac{y^5}{y^3}\) </item>
</list>
<para class="os-raise-text-bold">Answer:</para>
<para>\(\frac{y^5}{y^3} = (y)^{5-3} = (7)^2\)</para>
<list class="os-raise-noindent" start="8" type="enumerated">
  <item> \(\frac{r^4}{r}\) </item>
</list>
<para class="os-raise-text-bold">Answer:</para>
<para>\(\frac{r^4}{r} = (r)^{4-1} = (r)^3\)</para>
<list class="os-raise-noindent" start="9" type="enumerated">
  <item> \(\frac{q^9}{q^2}\) </item>
</list>
<para class="os-raise-text-bold">Answer:</para>
<para>\(\frac{q^9}{q^2} = (q)^{9-2} = (q)^7\)</para>
<list class="os-raise-noindent" start="10" type="enumerated">
  <item> \(\frac{(-u)^5}{(-u)^2}\) </item>
</list>
<para class="os-raise-text-bold">Answer:</para>
<para>\(\frac{(-u)^5}{(-u)^2} = (-u)^{5-2} = (-u)^3\)</para>
<list class="os-raise-noindent" start="11" type="enumerated">
  <item> \(\frac{(j)^6}{(j)^5}\) </item>
</list>
<para class="os-raise-text-bold">Answer:</para>
<para>\(\frac{(j)^6}{(j)^5} = (j)^{6-5} = (j)\)</para>
<list class="os-raise-noindent" start="12" type="enumerated">
  <item> \(\frac{(-z)^8}{(-z)^6}\) </item>
</list>
<para class="os-raise-text-bold">Answer:</para>
<para>\(\frac{(-z)^8}{(-z)^6} = (-z)^{8-6} = (-z)^{2}\)</para>
<title>Activity Synthesis</title>
<para>Ask students to summarize the Product Property and Quotient Property for Exponents in their own words. Add any
  missing details as needed. Emphasize that when multiplying like bases, the exponents are added, when dividing like
  bases, the exponents are subtracted. Stress that the base in these situations always remains the same.</para>
<title>5.1.2: Self Check </title>
<para class="os-raise-text-bold"><emphasis>After the activity, students will answer the following question to check their understanding of the
  concepts explored in the activity.</emphasis></para>
<para class="os-raise-text-bold">QUESTION:</para>
<para>Which of the following equations is correct?</para>
<table class="os-raise-textheavytable">
  <thead>
    <row>
      <entry scope="col" class="emphasis">Answers</entry>
      <entry scope="col" class="emphasis">Feedback</entry>
    </row>
  </thead>
  <tbody>
    <row>
      <entry> \(y^5 \cdot y^3=y^8\) </entry>
      <entry> That’s correct! Check yourself: The expression uses the Product Property for Exponents. To multiply
        with like bases, add the exponents. </entry>
    </row>
    <row>
      <entry> \(x^2 \cdot x^4=x^8\) </entry>
      <entry> Incorrect. Let’s try again a different way: The expression uses the Product Property for Exponents. To
        multiply with like bases, add the exponents, not multiply. The answer is \(y^5 \cdot y^3=y^8\). </entry>
    </row>
    <row>
      <entry> \(\frac{y^6}{y^3}=y^2\) </entry>
      <entry> Incorrect. Let’s try again a different way: This equation uses the Quotient Property for Exponents. To
        divide with like bases, subtract the exponents, not divide. The answer is \(y^5 \cdot y^3=y^8\). </entry>
    </row>
    <row>
      <entry> \(\frac{(-u)^8}{(-u)^2}=2(-u)^4\) </entry>
      <entry> Incorrect. Let’s try again a different way: This equation uses the Quotient Property for Exponents. To
        divide with like bases, subtract the exponents, not divide. The answer is \(y^5 \cdot y^3=y^8\). </entry>
    </row>
  </tbody>
</table>
<title>5.1.2: Additional Resources</title>
<title>Using Product and Quotient Properties for Exponents</title>
<para>When we combine like terms by adding and subtracting, we need to have the same base with the same exponent. But when
  you multiply and divide, the exponents may be different, and sometimes the bases may be different, too.</para>
<para>First, we will look at an example that leads to the Product Property.</para>
<para>What does \(x^2 \cdot x^3\) mean?</para>
<image src="https://k12.openstax.org/contents/raise/resources/c0cbb5db160126e5a9a9cb790a9e1bb56db91c2e"/><para>Notice that 5 is the sum of the exponents, 2 and 3. We see \(x^2 \cdot x^3\) is \(x^{2+3}\) or \(x^5\).</para>
<para>The base stayed the same, and we added the exponents. This leads to the <term data-schema-version="1.0" data-store="glossary-tooltip">Product Property for Exponents</term>.</para>
<note>
  <para class="os-raise-text-bold"> Product Property for Exponents </para>
    <hr/>
  <para>If \(a\) is a real number and \(m\) and \(n\) are integers, then</para>
  <para>\(a^m \cdot a^n=a^{m+n}\) </para>
  <para>To multiply with like bases, add the exponents.</para>
</note>
<para class="os-raise-text-bold">Example 1</para>
<para>\(4^3 \cdot 4^4 =4^{3+4}=4^7\)</para>
<para class="os-raise-text-bold">Example 2</para>
<para>\(12^2 \cdot 12^6 =12^{2+6}=12^8\)</para>
<para class="os-raise-text-bold">Example 3</para>
<para>\(y^7 \cdot 4^8 =y^{7+8}=y^{15}\)</para>
<para class="os-raise-text-bold">Example 4</para>
<para>\((2^b)^{10} \cdot (2b)^3 =(2b)^{10+3}=(2b)^{13}\)</para>
<para>Now we will look at an exponent property for division. As before, we’ll try to discover a property by looking
  at some examples.</para>
<list class="os-raise-noindent" type="enumerated">
  <item>Consider \(\frac{x^5}{x^2}\)</item>
</list>
<para><emphasis>Step 1 -</emphasis> What does it mean? </para>
<para><emphasis>Step 2 - </emphasis>Use the Equivalent Fractions Property. <m:math>
    <m:mfrac>
      <m:mrow>
        <m:menclose notation="updiagonalstrike">
          <m:mi>x</m:mi>
        </m:menclose>
        <m:mo> </m:mo>
        <m:mo>·</m:mo>
        <m:mo> </m:mo>
        <m:menclose notation="updiagonalstrike">
          <m:mi>x</m:mi>
        </m:menclose>
        <m:mo> </m:mo>
        <m:mo>·</m:mo>
        <m:mo> </m:mo>
        <m:mi>x</m:mi>
        <m:mo> </m:mo>
        <m:mo>·</m:mo>
        <m:mo> </m:mo>
        <m:mi>x</m:mi>
        <m:mo> </m:mo>
        <m:mo>·</m:mo>
        <m:mo> </m:mo>
        <m:mi>x</m:mi>
      </m:mrow>
      <m:mrow>
        <m:menclose notation="updiagonalstrike">
          <m:mi>x</m:mi>
        </m:menclose>
        <m:mo> </m:mo>
        <m:mo>·</m:mo>
        <m:mo> </m:mo>
        <m:menclose notation="updiagonalstrike">
          <m:mi>x</m:mi>
        </m:menclose>
      </m:mrow>
    </m:mfrac>
  </m:math>
</para>
<para><emphasis>Step 3 -</emphasis> Simplify.</para>
<list class="os-raise-noindent" start="2" type="enumerated">
  <item>Consider \(\frac{x^2}{x^3}\)</item>
</list>
<para><emphasis>Step 1 -</emphasis> What does it mean?</para>
<para><emphasis>Step 2 -</emphasis> Use the Equivalent Fractions Property.<m:math>
    <m:mfrac>
      <m:mrow>
        <m:menclose notation="updiagonalstrike">
          <m:mi>x</m:mi>
        </m:menclose>
        <m:mo> </m:mo>
        <m:mo>·</m:mo>
        <m:mo> </m:mo>
        <m:menclose notation="updiagonalstrike">
          <m:mi>x</m:mi>
        </m:menclose>
        <m:mo> </m:mo>
        <m:mo>·</m:mo>
        <m:mo> </m:mo>
        <m:mn>1</m:mn>
      </m:mrow>
      <m:mrow>
        <m:menclose notation="updiagonalstrike">
          <m:mi>x</m:mi>
        </m:menclose>
        <m:mo> </m:mo>
        <m:mo>·</m:mo>
        <m:mo> </m:mo>
        <m:menclose notation="updiagonalstrike">
          <m:mi>x</m:mi>
        </m:menclose>
        <m:mo> </m:mo>
        <m:mo>·</m:mo>
        <m:mo> </m:mo>
        <m:mi>x</m:mi>
      </m:mrow>
    </m:mfrac>
  </m:math>
</para>
<para><emphasis>Step 3 -</emphasis> Simplify.</para>
<para>Notice that in each case, the bases were the same and we subtracted exponents. We see \(\frac{x^5}{x^2}\) is
  \(x^{5-2}\) or \(x^3\). We see \(\frac{x^2}{x^3}\) is \(\frac1x\). When the larger exponent was in the numerator, we
  were left with factors in the numerator. When the larger exponent was in the denominator, we were left with factors in
  the denominator—notice the numerator of 1. When all the factors in the numerator have been removed, remember
  this is really dividing the factors to one, so we need a 1 in the numerator.
  <m:math>
    <m:mfrac>
      <m:menclose notation="updiagonalstrike">
        <m:mi>x</m:mi>
      </m:menclose>
      <m:mrow>
        <m:menclose notation="updiagonalstrike">
          <m:mi>x</m:mi>
        </m:menclose>
        <m:mo> </m:mo>
      </m:mrow>
    </m:mfrac>
    <m:mo>=</m:mo>
    <m:mn>1</m:mn>
  </m:math>
  . This leads to the <term data-schema-version="1.0" data-store="glossary-tooltip">Quotient
  Property for Exponents</term>. </para>
<note>
  <para class="os-raise-text-bold"> Quotient Property for Exponents </para>
    <hr/>
  <para>If \(a\) is a real number, \(a\neq0\), and \(m\) and \(n\) are integers, then</para>
  <para>\(\frac{a^m}{a^n}=a^{m-n}\), \(m&gt;n\) and \(\frac{a^m}{a^n}=\frac1{a^{n-m}}\), \(n&gt;m\)</para>
</note>
<para class="os-raise-text-bold">Example 5</para>
<para>\(7^5 \div 7^3 =7^{5-3}=7^2\)</para>
<para class="os-raise-text-bold">Example 6</para>
<para>\(k^9 \div k^2 =k^{9-2}=k^7\)</para>
<para class="os-raise-text-bold">Example 7</para>
<para>\(\frac {j^{10}}{j^7} =j^{10-7}=k^3\)</para>
<title>Try It: Using Product and Quotient Properties for Exponents</title>
<para>Simplify each expression.</para>
<list class="os-raise-noindent" type="enumerated">
  <item>\(12^2 \cdot 12^5\)</item>
  <item>\(20^{14} \div 20^4\)</item>
  <item>\(
    h^4 \cdot h^7\) </item>
  <item> \((-s)^2 \cdot (-s)^2\) </item>
  <item>\(\frac
    {b^7}{b^5}\)</item>
  <item> \({(-y)^6}{(-y)^3}\)</item>
  <item>\(\frac{x^3}{x^5}\)</item>
</list>
<para>Write down your answer. Then select the <emphasis>Solution</emphasis> button to compare your work. </para>
<title>Solution:</title>
<para>Compare your answers:</para>
<para>Here is how to simplify the expressions:</para>
<list class="os-raise-noindent" type="enumerated">
  <item>\(12^2 \cdot 12^5=12^7\)</item>
  <item>\(20^{14} \div 20^4=20^{10}\)</item>
  <item> \(h^4 \cdot h^7= h^{11}\) </item>
  <item>\(
    (-s)^2(-s)^2=(-s)^4
    \)</item>
  <item>\(\frac{b^7}{b^5} = b^2\) </item>
  <item>\((-y)^6(-y)^3 = (-y)^9\) </item>
  <item>\(\frac{
    x^3}{x^5}=\frac{1}{x^2}\) </item>
</list>
<title>Video: Understanding Product and Quotient Properties for Exponents</title>
<para>Watch the following video to learn more about applying the Product and Quotient Properties of Exponents.</para>
